# Who to notify about this repository.
#
# Added for #431, which needs "the codeowners" to resolve to somebody before an
# alarm can reach them. It is one person today - c902e34 wrote that down and
# guarded the two repository settings that quietly assumed otherwise - and that
# is the reason this is a file rather than a name inside a workflow: the day it
# stops being one person, nothing should need editing but this line.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO HERE. GitHub uses this to request review
# automatically on a pull request, and to resolve @mentions of the owning team.
# It does NOT, on its own, gate merging - that is branch protection's
# "Require review from Code Owners", declared in .github/expected-protections.yml
# and checked by protections-check.yml. With a single maintainer, requiring
# code-owner review would mean nobody can ever approve a pull request, which is
# exactly the class of setting c902e34 went and guarded.
#
# The alarm path this was added for does not read this file directly either:
# check-deployment.yml opens a tracking issue, and GitHub emails the people
# watching the repository. This file is what makes "the codeowners" a real list
# rather than a phrase, so that a second maintainer starts receiving those
# notifications by being added here.

* @jaimito-asuntos-gringuenos

# BELOW THIS LINE IS GENERATED, and the ordering is the point: CODEOWNERS is
# last-match-wins, so an organization's line has to come after the `*` rule
# above or the `*` rule overrides it and the organization owns nothing.
#
# `backend/app/core/codeowners.py` writes it from the roster: one line per
# claimed organization, naming its registry directory and the GitHub accounts
# its approved codeowners have linked. An organization whose codeowners have
# linked no account is LEFT OUT rather than given an empty line - a pattern
# with no owners after it overrides the `*` rule and requests nobody, which is
# worse than being covered by the maintainer.
#
# ONE THING THIS CANNOT DO. GitHub reads CODEOWNERS from a pull request's BASE
# branch, so a pull request that adds its own ownership line is not gated by
# it. An organization's first registry pull request therefore cannot also be
# the one granting it ownership: one pull request adds the line and the empty
# directory, and every registry change after that is gated by the owners it
# established. Doing both at once would leave the first import - the change
# that decides what an organization's trails are - as the one nobody at that
# organization has to approve.
#
# And the requirement this feeds is scoped to those directories on purpose.
# Turning on "Require review from Code Owners" repository-wide is the trap
# described above: with one maintainer it means nobody can ever approve a pull
# request. See .github/expected-protections.yml.

